 "voice": [
  {
   "id": "vo01",
   "t": 0.1,
   "dur": 4.741,
   "file": "vo/01.wav",
   "text": "How fast is this spark? The leading expert doubted it could ever be timed."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 5.9,
   "dur": 8.599,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "It's a nerve signal, racing down one cell's thin fibre. Your longest run from spine to toe: about a metre. How long does that take?"
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 15.5,
   "dur": 9.612,
   "file": "vo/03.wav",
   "text": "Johannes Müller thought it might be as fast as light. In eighteen fifty, his former student Helmholtz timed it. About thirty metres a second: cheetah speed."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo04",
   "t": 25.9,
   "dur": 14.226,
   "file": "vo/04.wav",
   "text": "It isn't current in a wire: the cell re-fires it, step by step. Many of yours wear fatty myelin, re-firing only at gaps, so the signal leaps. Where myelin is damaged, as in multiple sclerosis, signals can slow or stall."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo05",
   "t": 42.7,
   "dur": 8.389,
   "file": "vo/05.wav",
   "text": "The thinnest bare fibres crawl at about a metre a second. Wrapped ones, up to a hundred and fifty. Faster than a Formula One car."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo06",
   "t": 54.7,
   "dur": 5.799,
   "file": "vo/06.wav",
   "text": "So a stubbed toe can hurt twice. The jolt rides faster wires; the ache, bare ones."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo07",
   "t": 61.5,
   "dur": 5.929,
   "file": "vo/07.wav",
   "text": "Meanwhile, as you watched, a heart at rest beat about eighty times. Each began with a spark."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo08",
   "t": 68.3,
   "dur": 4.299,
   "file": "vo/08.wav",
   "text": "Next: the end of the line. A gap most signals can't jump."
  }
 ]
}